1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,871
This mouthwash was

2
00:00:00,871 --> 00:00:02,389
sold to scrub floors.

3
00:00:02,389 --> 00:00:03,852
Surgeons used it on wounds.

4
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:05,651
Janitors used it on floors.

5
00:00:05,651 --> 00:00:06,905
As a mouthwash?

6
00:00:06,905 --> 00:00:08,160
Barely anyone cared.

7
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:10,935
Around 1921, Gerard Lambert,

8
00:00:10,935 --> 00:00:12,177
the boss's son,

9
00:00:12,177 --> 00:00:13,701
needed people to want it.

10
00:00:13,701 --> 00:00:14,711
His team dug up

11
00:00:14,711 --> 00:00:17,484
an old medical word: halitosis.

12
00:00:17,484 --> 00:00:20,050
Bad breath wasn't a smell anymore.

13
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:21,710
It was a condition.

14
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:23,764
And your friends would never tell you.

15
00:00:23,764 --> 00:00:25,482
Then came Edna.

16
00:00:25,482 --> 00:00:27,759
Often a bridesmaid, never a bride.

17
00:00:27,759 --> 00:00:28,525
Her problem?

18
00:00:28,525 --> 00:00:29,802
Not her looks.

19
00:00:29,802 --> 00:00:30,686
Her breath.

20
00:00:30,686 --> 00:00:33,054
In seven years, revenue reportedly jumped

21
00:00:33,054 --> 00:00:36,646
from $115,000 to over $8 million.

22
00:00:36,646 --> 00:00:37,819
They didn't sell a cure.

23
00:00:37,819 --> 00:00:38,970
They sold the fear.
